  <div class="container">
    <section id="project">
      <h2>Project Description</h2>
      <p>
        <span class="highlight">CardQuest</span> is a planned digital card game where players use strategy,
        logic, and quick decision-making to win against opponents. At this stage, we are not building
        the playable version yet. We are focusing on the homepage that introduces the project idea clearly.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-cards">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What is it?</h3>
          <p>A strategy-based card game concept designed for students and young players who enjoy competition and creativity.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What problem does it solve?</h3>
          <p>Many students spend time on passive entertainment. Our idea offers a more interactive game that trains thinking and planning skills.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Why is it important?</h3>
          <p>Games can be more than fun. They can improve concentration, memory, and teamwork when designed with purpose.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>What makes it unique?</h3>
          <p>It combines a modern digital style with a classic card game concept and can later include original characters, levels, and story elements.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="functions">
      <h2>Functional Requirements</h2>
      <p>In the future, the product should include the following functions:</p>
      <ul class="feature-list">
        <li>User can open the game homepage and read about the project.</li>
        <li>User can learn the rules and main idea of the game.</li>
        <li>User can see character or card categories.</li>
        <li>User can create a player profile in the full version.</li>
        <li>User can start a match and collect points in the future version.</li>
        <li>User can see score, win/lose result, and progress.</li>
        <li>User can access updates and future improvements.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="technical">
      <h2>Technical Requirements</h2>
      <ul class="tech-list">
        <li><strong>Languages:</strong> HTML, CSS, JavaScript</li>
        <li><strong>Design:</strong> Responsive layout, clean structure, modern colors</li>
        <li><strong>Hosting:</strong> GitHub Pages or Railway</li>
        <li><strong>Repository:</strong> GitHub public repository</li>
        <li><strong>Future tools:</strong> JavaScript game logic, local storage, animations</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        For now, this page is the <strong>homepage/prototype</strong>. The interactive card game will be added later.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="prototype">
      <h2>Sketches / Prototype Design</h2>
      <p>
        This homepage acts as the first digital prototype of the project. It shows the visual style,
        structure, and information architecture of the future product.
      </p>
      <div class="prototype-box">
        <h3>Prototype Idea</h3>
        <p>Hero section → Project overview → Requirements → Interview → Authors → Timeline</p>
        <p>You can later replace this block with screenshots, hand-drawn sketches, or Canva/Figma design images.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="interview">
      <h2>Interview with Adylkhan Agay</h2>
      <p>
        To understand whether the card game idea is interesting and useful, we prepared a short interview with <strong>Adylkhan Agay</strong>.
      </p>

      <h3>Main Questions and Answers</h3>
      <ol class="interview-list">
        <li><strong>Why do card games attract students?</strong><br>
          Because they are simple to understand, competitive, and develop strategic thinking.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Can a card game be educational?</strong><br>
          Yes. If the game has rules, planning, and problem-solving elements, it can train memory, logic, and communication.
        </li>
        <li><strong>What is important in designing a good card game?</strong><br>
          The game should have clear rules, balanced cards, interesting visuals, and a fair scoring system.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Would students be interested in a digital card game?</strong><br>
          Yes, especially if it is easy to play, visually attractive, and gives players a chance to improve their skills.
        </li>
        <li><strong>What advice would you give to our team?</strong><br>
          Start with a clear concept, design the homepage well, and then build the game step by step.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div class="quote">
        “A good card game is not only about winning. It should also make players think, choose, and learn from every move.” — Adylkhan Agay
      </div>
    </section>

